===Noun===
{{en-noun|es}}

# A [[plant]] of the [[Brassicaceae]] [[family]], ''[[Raphanus sativus]]'' or [[Raphanus raphanistrum subsp. sativus|''Raphanus raphanistrum'' subsp. ''sativus'']], having an [[edible]] [[root]].
#* '''1659''' (indicated as '''1660'''), {{w|Robert Sharrock}}, “Of Propagation by Seed”, in ''The History of the Propagation & Improvement of Vegetables by the Concurrence of Art and Nature:'' [...], Oxford: Printed by A. Lichfield, printer to the [[w:University of Oxford|University]], for Tho[mas] Robinson, <small>{{OCLC|832949671}}</small>, [https://books.google.com/books?id=1QpFAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA15 pages 14–15]:
#*: Many times they ſow divers ſeeds in a Bed together, as '''Radiſhes''' and Carrots, that by ſuch time as the Carrots come up, the '''Radiſhes''' may be gone. Upon beds newly ſet with Licorice they ſow Onions or '''Radiſh''', or Lettice if their Licorice plants or ground be but weak, ſo as not quickly to cauſe a ſhadow with their leaves.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=J. R. Jackson|title=The New Vegetable (''Raphanus caudatus''.)|magazine=Nature and Art|location=London|publisher=Day & Son, Limited. 6 Gate Street, {{w|Lincoln's Inn Fields}}, (W.C.)|date=1 June 1866|volume=I|issue=I|page=8|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=oFYSAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA8|oclc=145376967|passage=The newly-introduced '''radish''', which has attracted the attention of horticulturists so much of late, is certainly a novelty, inasmuch as the edible portion of the plant is the seed-vessel, and not the root. The common '''radish''', in its numerous varieties, in such an exceedingly popular salad-plant, that we are scarcely prepared to look to this genus for new economic products or floral novelties. When we consider the many varieties of '''radish''' known to this country, from the long and tapering red-root to the white turnip-'''radish''', we might, in some measure, be prepared for a wider development of nature's laws in tropical countries.}}
# The root of this plant used as [[food]]. Some [[variety#Noun|varieties]] are [[pungent]] and usually eaten [[raw]] in [[salad]]s, etc., while others have a [[mild]]er [[taste]] and are [[cook#Verb|cooked]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Linda Gassenheimer|chapter=Veal Dinners|title=Dinner in Minutes: Memorable Meals for Busy Cooks|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|Houghton Mifflin Company]]|year=1993|page=290|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=l8IHP-8SEkwC&lpg=PT272|isbn=978-0-395-97181-9|passage=The flavors of radicchio and '''radish''' are delicious with only a touch of balsamic vinegar for a dressing.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|Mo Yan}} [pseudonym; Guan Moye]|author2={{w|Howard Goldblatt}}, transl.|title=Radish|series=Penguin Specials|location=Melbourne, Vic.|publisher=[[w:Penguin Books|Penguin Books Australia]]|year=2015|isbn=978-0-7343-1079-8|passage=Purple shoots had grown up from the wheat seeds sown in the furrows. He fell to his knees and dug up a '''radish'''. There was a sound like a bubble popping as the thin roots parted from the earth.}}
# With a [[distinguishing]] word: some other plant of the ''[[Raphanus]]'' [[genus]] or [[Brassicaceae]] family.
#: {{ux|en|{{l|en|rat-tail radish}} ({{taxlink|Raphanus caudatus|species|noshow=1|ver=170717}}'');'' {{l|en|wild radish}} (''{{ll|mul|Raphanus raphanistrum}}'')}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Margaret Plues|chapter=Cruciferæ|title=Rambles in Search of Wild Flowers, and How to Distinguish Them|location=London|publisher=Journal of Horticulture and Cottage Gardener Office, 162, {{w|Fleet Street}}, E.C.|year=1863|page=39|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=qoCe9vVNuq0C&pg=PA39|oclc=19134887|passage=The Wild '''Radish''' (Raphanus raphanistrum), is Edward's trophy, brought from a piece of waste ground near Hawkhurst, in Kent. The petals are white or pale lilac, veined distinctly with a deeper shade. The Sea '''Radish''' (R. maritimus,{{nb...}}), is primrose-coloured, also veined. Fanny brought it from the beautiful cliffs near Lizard Point.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Peter Burke|chapter=Seeds|title=Year-round Indoor Salad Gardening: How to Grow Nutrient-dense Soil-sprouted Greens in Less Than 10 Days|location=White River Junction, Vt.|publisher={{w|Chelsea Green Publishing}}|month=August|year=2015|page=56|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xS9mCgAAQBAJ&pg=PA56|isbn=978-1-60358-615-3|passage=Radishes were something new for me. The first time I tried growing for seed, I was surprised by the shape of the pods; they looked like hot peppers. Being the curious type I nibbled on a few pods and found they were delicious when still in the immature stage. I thought I had discovered something novel when, lo and behold, I found out that radishes grown in India just for their pods—a variety called Rat-Tail '''radish'''—for many years.}}

